146
00:05:59,260 --> 00:06:01,010
that I can use for my driver."

147
00:06:01,010 --> 00:06:03,405
That's the method
that we'll use for

148
00:06:03,405 --> 00:06:05,880
the scull device driver since
obviously that wouldn't be

149
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:07,920
something that you
would commonly have

150
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:11,345
in a Linux install.

151
00:06:11,345 --> 00:06:15,000
For a register and
unregister example,

152
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,570
in terms of what's actually

153
00:06:16,570 --> 00:06:19,095
happening in the Linux kernel,

154
00:06:19,095 --> 00:06:20,855
we're just going to
use the init and

155
00:06:20,855 --> 00:06:22,830
the cleanup functions to do

156
00:06:22,830 --> 00:06:27,905
the setup and tear
down for our device.

157
00:06:27,905 --> 00:06:31,380
What I recommend in
the LDD3 example,

158
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:34,175
take a look at the sleepy.c,
the sleepy module.

159
00:06:34,175 --> 00:06:38,710
This is the simplest version
that you can get in terms of

160
00:06:38,710 --> 00:06:40,620
how char device gets

161
00:06:40,620 --> 00:06:45,260
allocated based on an allocated
major and minor number.

162
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:47,795
Inside the sleepy_init,

163
00:06:47,795 --> 00:06:48,780
which is what we're
going to link to

164
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:50,490
our module_init function,

165
00:06:50,490 --> 00:06:52,840
we do a call to
register chardev,

166
00:06:52,840 --> 00:06:56,530
and we specify or we pass in

167
00:06:56,530 --> 00:07:00,375
the sleepy_major and
the name of the driver,

168
00:07:00,375 --> 00:07:02,070
and then a reference
to the f ops table,

169
00:07:02,070 --> 00:07:04,480
which we're going to
talk about in a minute.

170
00:07:04,580 --> 00:07:11,525
If we see a value of
zero in sleepy_major,

171
00:07:11,525 --> 00:07:13,445
then this means that we

172
00:07:13,445 --> 00:07:15,935
did not allocate a
