181
00:07:35,810 --> 00:07:40,475
What happens to the
character device if you

182
00:07:40,475 --> 00:07:42,220
register a character device with

183
00:07:42,220 --> 00:07:45,090
the kernel and then
you exit your module?

184
00:07:45,090 --> 00:07:49,805
This is important
conversation that

185
00:07:49,805 --> 00:07:52,140
we'll have often this semester

186
00:07:52,140 --> 00:07:55,430
when we're talking about
Linux kernel drivers.

187
00:07:55,430 --> 00:07:57,580
In a lot of cases, at least,

188
00:07:57,580 --> 00:08:01,030
you don't have the same kind

189
00:08:01,030 --> 00:08:02,190
of framework you've
got with user

190
00:08:02,190 --> 00:08:03,810
space for doing clean up.

191
00:08:03,810 --> 00:08:05,495
You're responsible for doing

192
00:08:05,495 --> 00:08:09,710
your own unregistering
when your module exits.

193
00:08:09,710 --> 00:08:11,250
You have to make sure
you're balancing.

194
00:08:11,250 --> 00:08:14,940
If you're using
register chardev call,

195
00:08:14,940 --> 00:08:16,320
you have to make
sure that you have

196
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:17,890
