197
00:08:17,890 --> 00:08:20,940
balanced.Innit and exit are

198
00:08:20,940 --> 00:08:22,730
typically the place where
that's going to get done.

199
00:08:22,730 --> 00:08:24,635
If you register your
chardev in innit,

200
00:08:24,635 --> 00:08:26,320
you want to make
sure you unregister

201
00:08:26,320 --> 00:08:28,360
your chardev in exit.

202
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:32,440
The device number is

203
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:34,085
represented in the Linux kernel

204
00:08:34,085 --> 00:08:36,345
with a typedef called dev_t.

205
00:08:36,345 --> 00:08:38,495
If you look at this
under the hood,

206
00:08:38,495 --> 00:08:42,400
it's really just a
uint32 unsigned integer.

207
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:45,045
In order to create a dev_t,

208
00:08:45,045 --> 00:08:48,890
you can use this macro
MKDEV int major, int minor.

209
00:08:48,890 --> 00:08:50,465
This is probably
something you've seen

210
00:08:50,465 --> 00:08:52,370
in other classes or

211
00:08:52,370 --> 00:08:54,400
other embedded
programs where we're

212
00:08:54,400 --> 00:08:56,555
just going to be using a
certain number of bits,

213
00:08:56,555 --> 00:08:59,735
20 bits for the minor
in the unsigned int,

214
00:08:59,735 --> 00:09:02,770
and we're going to be
masking in the major in

215
00:09:02,770 --> 00:09:06,815
the upper bits and the
minor in lower bits.

216
00:09:06,815 --> 00:09:09,010
We're storing two
different values

217
00:09:09,010 --> 00:09:10,155
in an unsigned integer,

218
00:09:10,155 --> 00:09:12,380
and then we can use
the major and minor to

219
00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:15,920
extract those values back
out of the unsigned integer.

220
00:09:16,320 --> 00:09:20,555
There's more
complicated examples

221
00:09:20,555 --> 00:09:22,625
that show you how to
register and unregister.

222
00:09:22,625 --> 00:09:24,350
One of them is the scull module

223
00:09:24,350 --> 00:09:25,655
that we mentioned before,

224
00:09:25,655 --> 00:09:28,150
that registers are a
range of devices and

225
00:09:28,150 --> 00:09:30,710
