231
00:09:42,935 --> 00:09:46,570
But as long as the driver
is written correctly,

232
00:09:46,570 --> 00:09:47,795
you need to be careful to handle

233
00:09:47,795 --> 00:09:50,115
that situation by
not just returning,

234
00:09:50,115 --> 00:09:52,580
you have to be cleaning up
whatever you did before.

235
00:09:52,580 --> 00:09:55,130
Because we did a register
chardev_region here,

236
00:09:55,130 --> 00:09:56,650
and because that
had already passed

237
00:09:56,650 --> 00:09:58,155
by the time we did the kmalloc,

238
00:09:58,155 --> 00:10:00,850
we have to have this goto
statement here to go to

239
00:10:00,850 --> 00:10:03,280
the fail step that actually

240
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:06,285
does the cleanup and is
going to do the unregister.

241
00:10:06,285 --> 00:10:08,315
This cleanup module
function can be

242
00:10:08,315 --> 00:10:11,450
handled for both innit and exit.

243
00:10:12,360 --> 00:10:22,045
The proc devices virtual
file is a place that is

244
00:10:22,045 --> 00:10:24,790
essentially a call in to

245
00:10:24,790 --> 00:10:27,805
the kernel that's going to
let the kernel tell us which

246
00:10:27,805 --> 00:10:30,400
