registered and which

247
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:34,525
device drivers are using
which major number.

248
00:10:34,525 --> 00:10:37,180
When we do our
dynamic registration,

249
00:10:37,180 --> 00:10:39,520
we'll know what our major
number is from the kernel

250
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:41,920
based on what registered
chardev gave us back.

251
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:44,440
But if I want to connect
to it from user space,

252
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:46,450
I won't be able to know what

253
00:10:46,450 --> 00:10:49,270
the driver could return back
from registered chardev.

254
00:10:49,270 --> 00:10:51,160
So cat proc devices is a way

255
00:10:51,160 --> 00:10:53,050
that I can tell from user space

256
00:10:53,050 --> 00:10:58,150
what my driver was actually
allocated as a major number.

257
00:10:58,150 --> 00:11:02,455
We're going to be able
to use this proc devices

258
00:11:02,455 --> 00:11:06,010
virtual file to
figure out how we

259
00:11:06,010 --> 00:11:09,595
can use a mknod to create
dev file system entries.

260
00:11:09,595 --> 00:11:11,620
Again, we can't create this in

261
00:11:11,620 --> 00:11:13,600
advance because we
won't know from

262
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:15,430
user space what is

263
00:11:15,430 --> 00:11:17,215
going to be allocated
when we load the driver.

264
00:11:17,215 --> 00:11:20,060
The driver is going to figure
that out during init time.

265
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:24,945
We'll load the driver
with its modern mod proc,

266
00:11:24,945 --> 00:11:27,210
and then we'll parse
proc devices after we

267
00:11:27,210 --> 00:11:30,410
load it to find the major
number that's assigned.

268
00:11:30,410 --> 00:11:33,190
There's a script that's
already written in

269
00:11:33,190 --> 00:11:36,070
the LDD3 repository
called module_load,

270
00:11:36,070 --> 00:11:38,110
that's a good example
about how to do this.

271
00:11:38,110 --> 00:11:39,610
There's some other
things that you probably

272
00:11:39,610 --> 00:11:41,050
want to do at init
time that are also

273
00:11:41,050 --> 00:11:44,440
covered in the
module_load script.

274
00:11:44,440 --> 00:11:46,090
Setting the owner and group.

275
00:11:46,090 --> 00:11:50,215
If you ran a script,
there's grep.

276
00:11:50,215 --> 00:11:51,400
If you want people
other than grep

277
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:52,585
to be able to access it,

278
00:11:52,585 --> 00:11:54,880
you could do that during
the module_load script.

279
00:11:54,880 --> 00:11:56,410
Then setting permissions who's

280
00:11:56,410 --> 00:11:59,020
able to read and
write, for instance.

281
00:11:59,020 --> 00:12:04,075
One of the script
that we'll use to

282
00:12:04,075 --> 00:12:09,295
parse the proc devices
virtual file is called AWK.

283
00:12:09,295 --> 00:12:11,650
It looks like an
acronym, but it's not,

284
00:12:11,650 --> 00:12:13,180
it's really just
an abbreviation of

285
00:12:13,180 --> 00:12:14,965
the original developer's names.

286
00:12:14,965 --> 00:12:16,390
This is a utility that's been

287
00:12:16,390 --> 00:12:17,965
around for a very long time.

288
00:12:17,965 --> 00:12:19,885
It's really useful when you have

289
00:12:19,885 --> 00:12:22,660
text output that's separated
by a common field,

290
00:12:22,660 --> 00:12:24,715
and one common field is space.

291
00:12:24,715 --> 00:12:26,830
Looking back at that
file that we had before,

292
00:12:26,830 --> 00:12:28,840
we see that we have
a number here,

293
00:12:28,840 --> 00:12:31,310
and then a space, and then
the name of the driver.

294
00:12:31,310 --> 00:12:34,325
AWK is pretty useful

295
00:12:34,325 --> 00:12:36,715
for solving this kind of problem

296
00:12:36,715 --> 00:12:38,855
in terms of parsing
out the major number.

297
00:12:38,855 --> 00:12:42,780
What we really want to do
is find a match for when

298
00:12:42,780 --> 00:12:46,005
this value equals
a specific device

299
00:12:46,005 --> 00:12:48,750
or driver name that
we want to match.

300
00:12:48,750 --> 00:12:52,850
Using the example of the
space disk driver or sd,

301
00:12:52,850 --> 00:12:56,620
if I wanted to parse this file
and figure out that sd was

302
00:12:56,620 --> 00:13:00,505
mapped to major number eight,

303
00:13:00,505 --> 00:13:02,965
I could use AWK in
a script like this,

304
00:13:02,965 --> 00:13:13,285
and I could pass in as
the module argument sd.

305
00:13:13,285 --> 00:13:15,205
What AWK would say then is,

306
00:13:15,205 --> 00:13:17,095
if field number 2 where there's

307
00:13:17,095 --> 00:13:20,230
the second space
separated field,

308
00:13:20,230 --> 00:13:22,000
if that matches sd,

309
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,280
then it's going to print
out the field number 1,

310
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:26,065
which would be in
this case, eight.

311
00:13:26,065 --> 00:13:28,390
That's basically what this
AWK line is telling us.

312
00:13:28,390 --> 00:13:31,960
It's saying to search

313
00:13:31,960 --> 00:13:34,645
proc devices for a match

314
00:13:34,645 --> 00:13:37,870
where the second field
matches the module name,

315
00:13:37,870 --> 00:13:39,505
and then print out
the first field.

316
00:13:39,505 --> 00:13:41,530
This is just going
to print out for us

317
00:13:41,530 --> 00:13:46,280
the eight value that
matches our major number.

318
00:13:46,980 --> 00:13:50,620
Here's an example of the
load script, and again,

319
00:13:50,620 --> 00:13:53,620
you can find the source for
this in the module_load

320
00:13:53,620 --> 00:13:59,095
inside the LDD3 repository
using link here on the slide.

321
00:13:59,095 --> 00:14:02,260
You start out with

322
00:14:02,260 --> 00:14:06,025
insmod to load the
module or use mode proc,

323
00:14:06,025 --> 00:14:08,980
and then you use
this AWK that we

324
00:14:08,980 --> 00:14:10,420
just talked about in
the previous slide to

325
00:14:10,420 --> 00:14:12,145
figure out what the
major number is.

326
00:14:12,145 --> 00:14:13,540
Then there's some logic here to

327
00:14:13,540 --> 00:14:16,360
remove if there already
is a device node,

328
00:14:16,360 --> 00:14:19,255
and then use mknod with the
major number that we found.

329
00:14:19,255 --> 00:14:20,230
We'll use minor number

330
00:14:20,230 --> 00:14:21,640
0 because we're just
going to access

331
00:14:21,640 --> 00:14:22,930
the first device that's

332
00:14:22,930 --> 00:14:25,555
created and will always
use character devices.

333
00:14:25,555 --> 00:14:27,040
That's what this line is doing.

334
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:28,780
Then you can change
the owner and

335
00:14:28,780 --> 00:14:31,150
group based on the
settings in the script.

336
00:14:31,150 --> 00:14:33,355
If you look at the example out,

337
00:14:33,355 --> 00:14:36,820
what you're going to get
when you run it normally,

338
00:14:36,820 --> 00:14:38,845
you'll see an output,
something like this.

339
00:14:38,845 --> 00:14:41,980
This was an example of
loading module_sleepy.

340
00:14:41,980 --> 00:14:45,550
We're going to be searching
through the proc devices list

341
00:14:45,550 --> 00:14:48,580
for a match for this
module names sleepy,

342
00:14:48,580 --> 00:14:50,005
figure out what the
major number is,

343
00:14:50,005 --> 00:14:52,255
and then setting up
this dev/sleepy node.

344
00:14:52,255 --> 00:14:56,455
After I run this, I can
see that cat proc devices,

345
00:14:56,455 --> 00:14:59,170
and grep for sleepy is going
to show me that sleepy

346
00:14:59,170 --> 00:15:02,350
got assigned number
241, major number.

347
00:15:02,350 --> 00:15:05,020
If I look in the /dev directory,

348
00:15:05,020 --> 00:15:07,555
I should see there are
script actually created,

349
00:15:07,555 --> 00:15:11,320
a device named dev sleepy that

350
00:15:11,320 --> 00:15:15,950
has a major number of 241
and a minor number of zero.